From: ai_re sonic-nixos <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] macsecmgrd: start an MKA session with the address the port will keep, and start it again when the address changes

wpa_supplicant reads the port's MAC address once, when the MKA session
starts. The KaY builds its SCI from it and uses it in the key server
election for as long as the session lives; nothing tells it about a later
change (the macsec_sonic driver has no address-change event).

macsecmgrd started the session as soon as STATE_DB PORT_TABLE said the port
was ready. A CONFIG_DB INTERFACE mac_addr is put on the netdev by intfmgrd
some time after that (found on an Edge-Core AS7772: 1.3 s later on the new
netdev of a stack restart), so the session ran with the switch address for
good while the port sent from the configured one. With both ends of a
cable on one switch (the reason for a per-port address in a lab) the two
KaYs then had the same address: "Cannot elect key server between me and
peer, duplicate MAC detected" / "duplicated SCI detected", and no SAs until
macsecmgrd was restarted by hand. With a peer on another switch the session
came up, with an SCI that is not the port's address.

- enableMACsec: besides the port being ready, the netdev must exist and,
  when CONFIG_DB INTERFACE|<port> has a mac_addr, have that address;
  otherwise the task is retried like any other condition (one NOTICE per
  wait). A port without a configured address starts at once, as before.
- The session remembers the address it was started with.
  followPortAddresses(), called from the main loop (at least once per
  SELECT_TIMEOUT), stops the session of a port whose netdev address is no
  longer that one and queues the port's CONFIG_DB entry again, so the same
  conditions and retries apply to the new session. Other ports are not
  touched.
- startWPASupplicant / stopWPASupplicant are virtual so that the unit tests
  can count them; tests in tests/mock_tests/macsecmgrd.
---
